  m_Name: L22_Gobeklitepe
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Cutrium.Presentation::Cutrium.Presentation.Landmark.LandmarkDefinition
  _landmarkId: gobeklitepe
  _displayTitle: Gobekli Tepe
  _shortDescription: Gobekli Tepe's T-shaped stone pillars, carved with animals,
    date back to around 9600 BC. Older than pottery and settled farming communities,
    this monumental site transformed how we understand human history. The lack of
    clear domestic traces in its circular structures, whose pillars can exceed five
    metres, suggests that the site may have hosted large gatherings and rituals.
  _sector: Sanliurfa / TURKEY
  _displayTitleTurkish: "G\xF6beklitepe"
  _shortDescriptionTurkish: "G\xF6beklitepe\u2019nin hayvan kabartmal\u0131 T bi\xE7imli
    ta\u015F s\xFCtunlar\u0131 yakla\u015F\u0131k M\xD6 9600\u2019e kadar uzan\u0131r.
    Bu an\u0131tsal alan, \xE7anak \xE7\xF6mlekten ve yerle\u015Fik tar\u0131m toplumlar\u0131ndan
    bile daha eski olmas\u0131yla insanl\u0131k tarihine bak\u0131\u015F\u0131 de\u011Fi\u015Ftirdi.
    Baz\u0131lar\u0131 be\u015F metreyi a\u015Fan s\xFCtunlar\u0131n bulundu\u011Fu
    dairesel yap\u0131larda belirgin ev izlerine rastlanmamas\u0131, alan\u0131n
    b\xFCy\xFCk bulu\u015Fmalar ve rit\xFCeller i\xE7in kullan\u0131lm\u0131\u015F
    olabilece\u011Fini d\xFC\u015F\xFCnd\xFCr\xFCr."
  _sectorTurkish: "\u015Eanl\u0131urfa / T\xDCRK\u0130YE"
  _artwork: {fileID: 9132063075479195388, guid: faf9552e4edc66a40894ad2b2154029e, type: 3}
